</subsection>

  <subsection>
    
    <title> Limits of Sequences</title>
    <p>
      An important thing we want to know about infinite sequences is what happens to the sequence as the terms approach infinity.
    </p>
    <p>
      If <md>\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} a_n = L</md> where <m>L</m> is a real number, then the sequence is called convergent.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the limit does not exist or approaches infinity, then the sequence is called divergent.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is similar to convergent or divergent improper integrals that you encountered in Calculus II (Math 152).
    </p>
    <p>
      For the most part you can calculate these limits at infinity, using the tools you learned in Calculus I for the limits of functions, things like the limit laws,
      the squeeze theorem, l'Hospital's rule and known long-run behavior of specific functions.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Long-run Behavior of Arctan</title>
      <p>
      <md> \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \arctan(2n) = \frac{\pi}{2} </md> 
      </p>
      <p>
        This sequence converges since the long-run behavior of the arctan function tends towards <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
      </p>
    </example>
<example>
  <title>l'Hopital's Rule</title>
  
  
      <p>
      <md> \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \frac{3+5n^2}{n+n^2} = 5 </md> 
      </p>
      <p>
        This sequence converges since the long-run behavior of a rational function tends towards the ratio of its leading coefficients. Two applications of
        l'Hopital's rule will produce the same result.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>Squeeze Theorem</title>
      
      
      <p>
      <md> \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \frac{\cos^2(n)}{2^n}= 0 </md> 
      </p>
      <p>
        The limit of this sequence can be found using the squeeze theorem. Since <m>0\leq \cos^2(n) \leq 1</m>, we can trap the sequence <m>O \leq \frac{\cos^2(n)}{2^n}
          \leq \frac{1}{2^n}</m> and since <m>\frac{1}{2^n}</m> tends towards zero, the sequence is also squeezed down to zero.
      </p>
    </example>
<example>
  <title>Limit Laws</title>
  
  
      <p>
      <md> \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \sqrt{\frac{n+1}{9n+1}}= \frac{1}{3} </md> 
      </p>
      <p>
        Using the limit law that says we can pull the limit inside the square root, we can evaluate the limit inside the square root to get 1/9 and then take the square 
        root of 1/9 to get 1/3.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>An Unbounded Sequence</title>
      
      
      <p>
      <md> \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} (6n-5) = \infty </md> 
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the sequence grows without bound, this sequence diverges.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      A way of determining if a sequence converges that is unique to sequences is called the bounded-monotonic sequence property. If a sequence is bounded, where all of the 
      terms are trapped between two numbers (similar to the squeeze theorem) and if the sequence is monotonic, meaning that it is always increasing or always decreasing,
      then that sequence must converge. Think about it for a minute to figure out why this is true.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>A Bonunded, Monotonic Sequence</title>
      
      
      <p>
        Consider the sequence <m>a_n = \frac{3^{n+2}}{5^{n}}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first few terms of the sequence are <m>\frac{9}{5}, \frac{27}{25}, \frac{81}{125}, \frac{243}{625}, ...</m> if we start the sequence at <m>n=1</m>. The sequence is bounded 
        above by <m>\frac{9}{5}</m> and below by zero. Terms are always getting smaller, so the sequence is monotonically decreasing. Therefore, the sequence must converge.
        Taking the limit gives zero since the can be written as <m>a_n = 9(3/5)^n</m> which is a geometric series with a common ratio less than one.
      </p>
      </example>
      <example>
        <title>A Bounded Sequence that Diverges</title>
        
        
        <p>
          Consider the sequence <m>a_n = \cos(\pi n) = 1, -1, 1, -1,...</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          The sequence is clearly bounded by -1 and 1. However, there is no way to find the limit at infinity since the sequence continues to oscillate between -1 and 1 
          without ever "settling down" a single value.
        </p>
      </example>
      <example>
        <title>A Non-Montonic Sequence that Converges</title>
        <p>
          Consider the sequence: <m>a_n = \frac{(-3)^n}{n!} = 1, -3, \frac{9}{2}, \frac{-27}{6},...</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since the sequence oscillates between positive and negative values, the sequence also oscillates between increasing and decreasing, so the sequence is not
          monotonic. The function is also bounded between some positive and negative values. (-100 and 100 will work.) However, in the long-run, the denominator grows at a
          faster rate than the numerator and the sequence converges to zero.
        </p>
      </example>
      <p>
        Here are a few more videos with further examples.
      </p>
